<article class="post-body resource">
  <p>The word <em>gospel</em> means &ldquo;good news.&rdquo; But good news only lands on a person who knows the bad news first. So let me start there.</p>

  <p>God is holy. We are not. Every one of us has sinned and fallen short of His glory (Romans 3:23), and sin is not a smudge to be wiped off. Scripture calls it a debt, and it uses that word on purpose. It is a debt none of us can pay. No amount of effort, religion, or self-improvement closes the distance between a holy God and a man like me.</p>

  <p>That is where Jesus comes in.</p>

  <p>The gospel is the announcement of who Jesus is and what He did. Jesus, the eternal Son of God, lived the perfect life we could never live. He kept the law of God from the inside out, without a single failure. Then He went to the cross and absorbed the full penalty our sin deserved. Three days later He walked out of the tomb, and death has never recovered. In His sinless life God supplied the obedience the law demands. In His death God removed the curse the law pronounces. God provides what God requires.</p>

  <p>Here is the part that still stops me. God declares you righteous while you are still a sinner. He does not wait for you to clean yourself up. He takes His own Son&rsquo;s righteousness and credits it to your account the moment you trust in Christ alone. R.C. Sproul said it as plainly as it can be said: &ldquo;We are justified not by our works, not by our efforts, not by our deeds, but by faith, and by faith alone.&rdquo;</p>

  <p>So this is not a self-improvement program, and it is not religion. Every religion on earth is a ladder we climb toward God. The gospel is God climbing down to us. It is a rescue: planned by the Father, accomplished by the Son, received by faith. Nothing in your hands. Everything in His.</p>

  <p>If you have never placed your trust in Jesus, the invitation is open to you right now, wherever you happen to be sitting. Admit that you cannot save yourself. Believe that Christ died in your place and rose again. Trust Him, and Him alone, as your Lord and Savior.</p>

  <p><em>That</em> is the gospel. And if it has just become yours, I would love to hear about it. Write me at <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.</p>

